Field-label playbook for this lane (use the RULE to spell any rejected synthetic label; resolve with `hctf labels`):
FIRST check each question's `answer_format.kind`. If it is `enum`, the GM HANDS you the closed vocabulary in `accepted_values` — pick the right one from that list, NEVER probe or guess its spelling (role, scenario, and true/false fields are all enums). If it is `mitre_attack_id`, submit a `T####`/`T####.###` id (literal `none` only if permitted). The HARD labels below are always `exact_text` (no list given) — those are the ones that need the derivation rule + `hctf labels`.
Field-label playbook for this lane's exact_text labels (generator vocabulary — match the RULE, do not guess synonyms; resolve with `hctf labels` which already seeds from these):
- **control**: CLOSED vocab, named after the cmd grammar on the executed path: `direct` (no construct), `errorlevel_guard` (`if errorlevel`), `goto_dispatch` (`goto :LABEL`), `call_subroutine` (`call :LABEL`), `if_guard` (`if defined X` / any `if` test). Name by the IF/GOTO/CALL keyword, never a descriptive synonym. VBScript (construct wrapping the Decode_<id>() call in Recover_<id>): `direct`, `select_case` (Select Case 1), `do_once` (Do .. Exit Do .. Loop), `while_once` (While p<1 .. Wend); an outer `If True Then` block and single-line opaque `If (2 > 1) Then` junk are noise. VBS `On Error Resume Next` .. Decode_ .. `On Error GoTo 0` (NOT direct / on_error_resume, both GM-rejected) is `error_guard` — the VBS twin of Batch `errorlevel_guard`: it checks Err after the Decode call and bails (GM-confirmed Day 4 TT+Mastery). VBS `For p = 0 To 0` -> `for_once` (GM-confirmed D5/D6). Confirmed: direct, errorlevel_guard, goto_dispatch, call_subroutine, if_guard, select_case, do_once, while_once, for_once, error_guard. Already REJECTED (never resubmit): dead_label, defined_flag, defined_guard, do_exit, do_loop, double_pipe, environment_probe, flag_guard, for_loop, goto, if defined, if_defined, if_defined_guard, linear. e.g. `if defined READY ..`->if_guard; `if errorlevel 1`->errorlevel_guard; VBS Do..Exit Do->do_once; VBS While p < 1..Wend->while_once.
- **representation**: material representation = field 1 of the declared codec (`Codec_<id>`/`CODEC_<id> = "rep|transform|key"`) copied VERBATIM -- never re-describe it. Spelling grammar: <decimal|hex>_<separator name> (pipe/dash/colon/comma/dot/space/csv), hex_compact/hex_pairs/hex_escape/hex_literal/percent_hex, base64_literal/base64_chunks/urlsafe_base64, integer_list/integer_tuple, reversed_<x> = loaded in reverse. `lib.codec.REPS` lists every name seen D3-D6. Confirmed: decimal_pipe, decimal_dash, hex_compact, hex_colon, reversed_decimal, base64_literal, base64_chunks, hex_dash, decimal_comma, reversed_hex.
- **transform**: reversible byte transform = field 2 of the declared codec, copied VERBATIM, named for the ENCODE side (subtract_modulo_256 decodes by ADDING the key). `lib.codec.TRANSFORMS` lists every name seen D3-D6. Confirmed: subtract_modulo_256, repeating_key_xor, rolling_xor, single_byte_xor, reverse_bytes, reverse_then_xor, identity, add_modulo_256.
- **expansion**: CLOSED vocab, decided by the fragment POOL NAME + the JOIN form in :HUNTRESS_INSPECT (mined from every GM-verified D3-D6 single/TT case, 0 conflicts in 545): fragments named `h<N>` AND joined with `call set` (bare or inside `for %%G in (..) do call set`) = `call_double` (33/33); a `for %%G in (..) do call set` join over any OTHER pool (v_a/item/fragment/FrAg..) = `for_indirect` (122/122); everything else = `delayed` (390/390) -- including h-pools joined with `!h0!!h1!..` (why D6's h-pools were delayed). EXCEPT contract run_for_time:false (D15 regular untimed pool, 54/54) AND every attempt started on/after 2026-10-16 (D16 single + TT, run_for_time ABSENT, 18/18; `delayed` for a %-join REJECTED on the D16 single): the join STATEMENT alone -- FOR = `for_indirect` over ANY pool, `call set` over h = `call_double`, other `call set` = `percent`, `!x!` = `delayed`, `%x%` = `percent` (K.BATCH_EXPANSION_STATEMENT_RULE). Confirmed (CLOSED set): percent, delayed, call_double, for_indirect. Already REJECTED (never resubmit): call-double, double_call, immediate, percent_expansion. e.g. h0..h3 + call set->call_double; h0..h3 + !h0!!h1!->delayed; for %%G .. call set over v_a/item->for_indirect.
- **style**: JScript code-style labels (closed sets in the question text; GM-confirmed D2-D6): FUNCTION-style of the named function: `function f(` = declaration, `var f = function(` = anonymous_expression, `var f = function f_body(` = named_expression. INVOCATION-style = how the recover/inspect body invokes the next top-level function: plain `f(x)` = direct, `f.call(..)` (also via a local `invoke` helper that does `block.call`) = call, `f.apply(..)` = apply. PROPERTY-style of the descriptor reads: only `o.k` = dot, only `o["k"]` = bracket_literal, both = mixed. Confirmed (CLOSED set): declaration, named_expression, anonymous_expression, direct, call, apply, dot, bracket_literal, mixed. e.g. var decode_x = function decode_x_body(s)->named_expression; invoke.call(null, decode_x, s)->call; o.steps + o["adapter"]->mixed.
- **attack**: MITRE ATT&CK technique id from the step-graph ROLE. Execution roles map to the host language: batch launcher/staged_chain=T1059.003, JScript launcher/staged_chain=T1059.007, VBScript launcher/staged_chain==T1059.005, bash=T1059.004, python=T1059.006, powershell=T1059.001, Node.js=T1059.007 (JavaScript, like JScript). A graph ending in a handoff is `staged_chain` even if next_stage is a .js file (not 'relay'). Non-execution roles map by ROLE, never by adapter: persistence_descriptor (build_schedule>register_descriptor) = T1053.003 even with a systemd_descriptor/npm_lifecycle adapter; bash advanced_syntax (recognize_syntax>reconstruct_intent) = T1059.004 (Day 7-9). v=3 cross-language plans (PHP/WSF/HTA D8, Lua/SCT/OpenResty D9) STATE attack=: the PARENT ids T1059/T1053, verbatim. Already REJECTED (never resubmit): T1005, T1027.009, T1059.007, T1105, T1119, T1218.010, T1560, T1620. e.g. batch launcher->T1059.003; jscript staged_chain->T1059.007; vbscript launcher->T1059.005; downloader/stager->T1105; dropper->T1140; uploader->T1041; impact->T1486; nodejs launcher->T1059.007; nodejs persistence_descriptor->T1053.003.
Generator naming LAWS — when an exact_text label is REJECTED, DERIVE the next spelling from these; do NOT brainstorm descriptive synonyms (every synonym below was GM-rejected):
- `<keyword>_guard`: a conditional / error bail-out is named by the SHORT language keyword that opens it — batch `if errorlevel`->errorlevel_guard, batch `if defined`->if_guard, VBS `On Error Resume Next`->error_guard. The spelled-out statement is ALWAYS wrong (if_defined, on_error_resume, defined_guard, flag_guard rejected).
- `<keyword>_once`: a run-once loop = loop keyword + `_once` — Do..Exit Do->do_once, While..Wend->while_once, `For p=0 To 0`->for_once (do_exit/while_wend/for_next rejected).
- control transfer = `<keyword>_<action>`: `goto :L`->goto_dispatch, `call :L`->call_subroutine.
- format = container[_runtime]: native = container only (elf/pe/macho); managed = container + the real runtime-spec token (.NET PE = pe_cli, from PE + ECMA-335 CLI); msil/dotnet/managed/cil all rejected.
- representation = `<numbase>_<separator-name>` (decimal_pipe, hex_colon) or `<base64|hex>_<variant>`; transform is named for the ENCODE direction (subtract_modulo_256 decodes by ADDING).
- ATT&CK execution role scales by host language: launcher/staged_chain -> T1059.{003 batch, 004 bash, 005 vbscript, 006 python, 007 jscript/node.js, 001 powershell}; downloader/stager T1105, dropper T1140, uploader T1041, impact T1486, persistence_descriptor T1053.003 (by role, not adapter).
- A CLASSIFICATION of how/where something is stored is a 2-3 token compound <form>_<home>_<kind>: raw_hcfg_section, raw_const_array, wasm_data_segment (D17). When siblings' ONE-word label covers the simple branch (base64), the other branch is a QUALIFIED compound: every 1-2 token piece (raw, raw_hcfg, hcfg_section, raw_const, const_array) was rejected. Go to depth 3 (`hctf labels --compose`) before inventing new words.
- Literal-grounding ("the answer must appear in the bytes") holds for IDENTIFIER values only (symbols, paths, strings, names, hashes). Classification labels are the GM's own concept words: `section`/`array`/`segment` never occur in the file. Do not prune them for being absent from the artifact.

Do this:
1. Read the failing `variant-*` case's artifacts and contract. For EACH answer_id, derive the correct value
   independently from the bytes (decode/parse/decrypt/compute). Do not assume the solver's current value is right.
2. Record those derived answers in `challenges/day23/scripting-press-start-to-decode/solver/NOTES.md` (NOT in `expected.json` -- it is GM-verified only and the
   harness restores any edit), then generalize `solve.py` to reproduce them AND still pass every other case — detect structure, never hard-code
   this variant's values. Reuse `lib/*` but do not edit it (other workers import it concurrently): list new helpers and
   confirmed GM vocabulary under `## Proposed lib additions` in `challenges/day23/scripting-press-start-to-decode/solver/NOTES.md`; the post-run ratchet merges them.
   ALWAYS time-box every solver run (`timeout 5 python3 solve.py ...` / `timeout 20 hctf solver-test`): a too-slow
   solver ran unbounded for 78 s in one generalize. If the failure is SPEED: profile (`python3 -X importtime`,
   `time`), remove brute-force search per set, decode once -- target < 1 s for all sets (a generic fallback that runs ONLY when
   the direct parse finds nothing costs nothing on the happy path: keep it).
   The case artifacts are AES zips (password `infected`): `7z x -pinfected` or `lib.common.read_member`.
3. `hctf solver-test script-analysis-day-23-game-resource-loader` must pass for EVERY case, and `hctf solver-run script-analysis-day-23-game-resource-loader/time_trial` must output every answer_id —
   then STOP immediately (the timed mode is waiting on you; no extra hardening, no synthetic cases).
4. The solver runs ON THE HOMEBASE VM next to the Game Master under a short clock (~6-7s for the whole
   download+solve+submit), so keep it FAST and dependency-light. Artifacts are already downloaded for you
   (in parallel) before the solver runs — don't fetch anything yourself. For a compounded Mastery bundle the
   GM ships ONE download per set, often all named the same; pair each set to its bytes by the contract's
   `artifacts[].sha256`, not by file name (use `lib.common.container_by_sha` / `delivery_missing`), or you will
   answer set 2 from set 1's bytes. Import only: stdlib, `lib.*`, pyelftools, pefile, capstone, pycryptodome,
   pyzipper, androguard, droidasc, binary-refinery (`lib.codec` decodes every declared script codec). The only
   subprocess allowed is `node` (JScript vm sandbox, like `hctf/js_sandbox.js`) -- no 7z/jadx/apt tools.
5. Do NOT submit and do NOT start/retry any mode — the orchestrator retries with a fresh variant once you exit.
   Add ONE line to `challenges/day23/scripting-press-start-to-decode/solver/NOTES.md` starting `VARIANT AXIS:` (what differed in this variant vs the cases that passed, and the parse rule that covers it) -- the post-run ratchet collects these into the technique log. The harness ends your session the moment the
   failing case passes with no regressions — don't spend time on extra robustness checks after the fix.

Patch safely: use the Edit tool (non-unique matches are refused) or `assert s.count(old) == 1` before `s.replace(old, new)`;
a non-unique replace silently patches a sibling function. Profiling: `python3 -m cProfile -s cumtime solve.py ... 2>&1 | head -40`
(redirect stderr INTO the pipe -- `2>&1 >/dev/null` sends the profile to /dev/null).
Speed: a Mastery attempt runs every set inside one clock -- if per-set work is heavy, solve the sets in parallel
processes (`lib.common.group_by_set` + fork `ProcessPoolExecutor`, see the skeleton's `main()` in hctf/templates/solve_skeleton.py).
Never edit any `cases/*/expected.json`: expected answers are GM-verified only (the harness restores any change). A
`variant-*` case with an empty or partial expected.json is judged by FILLING every field, not by matching values.
